WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
Authorization answers the question, is this identity allowed to perform this action on this resource right now.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.800
It must be enforced on the server, close to the operation that matters.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:21.580
User interface hiding, disabled buttons, and client-side route checks may improve usability, but they are not enough to protect data or privileged behavior.

4
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:29.140
C sharp and ASP.NET Core applications commonly use role-based and policy-based authorization.

5
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.000
Roles can be useful when they match real business responsibilities.

6
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:41.820
Policies are often better when access depends on claims, ownership, tenant, workflow state, environment, or operation context.

7
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.360
The model should fit the business rule, not the other way around.

8
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.120
Object-level authorization is critical.

9
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.060
A user may be allowed to view invoices, but not every invoice.

10
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.560
A support operator may update some customer records, but not all fields.

11
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:01.140
A service may process one tenant's messages, but not another tenant's data.

12
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:07.720
Authorization checks should include the specific object or action, not only the general feature.

13
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:17.920
Least privilege applies to users, services, administrators, background workers, database accounts, cloud identities, and deployment pipelines.

14
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:24.260
Broad permission may feel convenient during implementation, but it creates larger consequences when mistakes happen.

15
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.760
High-risk administrative and support workflows should be especially visible in review.

16
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.800
Avoid scattering permission checks across unrelated code in ways that are hard to audit.

17
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:42.900
Central policies, resource-based handlers, service-layer checks, and consistent authorization patterns make it easier to reason about access.

18
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.760
Reviewers should be able to locate where the decision is made and understand what data the decision depends on.
